.TH PACHUD 1 "2026-11-10" "pachud 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pachud \- event-driven HUDs and notification daemon for Wayland
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pachud
.br
.B pachud
.I command
[\fIargument\fR ...]
.br
.B pachud
.RB [ \-h " | " \-\-help " | " \-V " | " \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pachud
sleeps until something deserves attention, then shows a small floating HUD on a Wayland compositor
that implements wlr-layer-shell.
It reacts to volume and brightness changes, low battery, memory pressure, network state changes,
timers and alarms, and desktop notifications, and it replaces the notification daemon (mako, dunst, ...).
.PP
Run without arguments it is the daemon.
With a command it is a client of the running daemon, which it reaches through the socket
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pachud.sock .
HUDs cannot be clicked: they are closed and acted on with these commands, typically from compositor keybinds.
.PP
Alerts (warning and critical toasts, timers, battery and memory alerts, every notification card)
are queued and never lost.
Sliders and informational toasts take the screen briefly and the alert they interrupted comes back.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR toast " [" \-l " " info | warn | crit "] [" \-t " \fISECONDS\fR] \fITEXT\fR..."
Show a one-line message.
.B warn
and
.B crit
are alerts and queue.
.TP
.BI slider " PERCENT LABEL" ...
Show a level bar.
.TP
.BI timer " DURATION " [ LABEL "...]"
Start a timer.
.I DURATION
is made of numbers with a unit, such as
.BR 90s ", " 5m ", " 1h30m
(units
.BR s ", " m ", " h ", " d ;
at most 30 days).
A unit is required.
Prints the new id.
.TP
.BR alarm " [" \-r " \fIRECUR\fR] [\fIYYYY-MM-DD\fR] \fIHH:MM\fR [\fILABEL\fR...]"
Set an alarm for the next such wall-clock time, today or tomorrow, or, with a date, for that moment.
.I RECUR
is
.BR daily ", " weekdays ", " weekends
or a list such as
.BR mon,wed,fri .
A recurring alarm stays scheduled after it fires.
A dated alarm cannot repeat.
.TP
.BR snooze " [\fIDURATION\fR]"
Re-arm the timer or alarm that last went off as a new one-shot (default 5m, same label).
.TP
.BI pause " ID"
Freeze a timer's remaining time.
.TQ
.BI resume " ID"
Continue a paused timer.
.TQ
.BI extend " ID DURATION"
Add time to a timer or alarm (to the time left, if paused).
.TP
.B list
Show timers and alarms with their ids, time left and recurrence.
.TP
.BR cancel " \fIID\fR | " all
Cancel one or all.
.TP
.B dismiss
Close what is on screen and show the next queued item.
.TP
.BR dnd " [" on " [\fIDURATION\fR] | " off " | " toggle " | " status ]
Do not disturb.
While on, notification cards below critical urgency are not shown and make no sound; they are counted
and the total is reported when it is switched off.
Timers, alarms, battery and memory alerts and sliders are not affected.
The state is kept in memory only.
.TP
.BR action " [\fIN\fR]"
Invoke action
.I N
(default 1) of the notification card on screen: the application is told, and the card is closed.
Cards list their actions as numbered hints, for example
.BR "[1] Reply  [2] Archive" .
.PP
Commands exit with status 0 on success, 1 if the daemon refused the request (the reason is printed to
standard error) or cannot be reached, and 2 for a usage error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PACHUD_OUTPUT
Show HUDs on this monitor (for example
.BR DP-1 ).
Unset lets the compositor choose.
An unknown name is reported once, with the names that exist.
.TP
.B PACHUD_SCALE
Pin the render scale, 1 to 4, fractions allowed.
By default the compositor's preferred scale is followed, per monitor.
.TP
.B PACHUD_FADE_MS
Fade duration in milliseconds (default 120).
.B 0
disables the fade.
.TP
.B PACHUD_PROBE_HOST
An IPv4 address to ping for the network slowdown alert.
Without it no probe is made.
.TP
.B PACHUD_PROBE_INTERVAL
Seconds between probes, 5 to 3600 (default 30).
.TP
.B PACHUD_STARTUP_GRACE
Seconds after start-up during which a memory or battery alert that is not worse than one shown in the
last 30 minutes is not shown again (default 60;
.B 0
disables it).
.TP
.B PACHUD_SOUND
.B 0
turns all sound off.
.TP
.B PACHUD_PLAYER
Program started with a sound file as its only argument (default
.BR pw-play ,
else
.BR paplay ).
.TP
.BR PACHUD_ALARM_SOUND ", " PACHUD_NOTIFY_SOUND ", " PACHUD_ALERT_SOUND
Sound for timers and alarms (default
.BR alarm-clock-elapsed ),
for notification cards, and for battery and memory alerts (both off by default):
an absolute file path or a name from the freedesktop sound theme.
Empty or
.B 0
means silent.
.TP
.B LANGUAGE ", " LANG ", " LC_MESSAGES
Select the language of the HUD messages.
.TP
.B PULSE_SERVER
A
.B unix:
socket of the sound server to use instead of the default.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Where timers are saved.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pachud.sock
The daemon's command socket.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/pachud/alerts
The last memory and battery alert shown, for the start-up grace period.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/pachud/timers
Timers and alarms, restored when the daemon starts (default
.IR ~/.local/state/pachud/timers ).
Ones that came due while the daemon was not running fire once.
.TP
.I ~/.config/gtk-3.0/settings.ini
The icon theme to look notification icons up in, tried before hicolor and Adwaita.
.SH NOTIFICATIONS
pachud implements the
.B org.freedesktop.Notifications
service, so only one notification daemon can run at a time.
It supports summary and body (tags removed), urgency, replacement by id, sound hints, actions,
and icons as raw pixels, as PNG paths or as icon-theme names (PNG only).
.PP
Cards stay up 3 s (low urgency), 6 s (normal) or 15 s (critical); an explicit timeout is honoured between
1.5 and 30 s.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
pachud timer 5m tea
pachud alarm \-r weekdays 07:30 gym
pachud alarm 2026\-12\-25 07:30 present
pachud snooze 10m
pachud dnd on 1h
pachud action 2
.fi
.PP
In Hyprland, for instance:
.nf
hl.bind("SUPER + Escape", hl.dsp.exec_cmd("pachud dismiss"))
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pw\-play (1),
.BR paplay (1),
.BR notify\-send (1)
.SH BUGS
Battery, charger and backlight events have only been exercised on a desktop machine without a battery
or backlight.
TCP sound servers are not supported.
